HTML (HyperText Markup Language) and JavaScript are two different programming languages that are commonly used together in web development.

HTML is a markup language used for structuring and presenting content on the web. It consists of a set of tags (also called elements) that define the structure, layout, and meaning of web content. HTML tags provide instructions to web browsers on how to display text, images, links, and other elements on a web page.

For example, the following HTML code creates a heading element with the text "My Heading":

```html
<h1>My Heading</h1>
```

HTML does not have any built-in functionality for interactivity, animation, or dynamic content. It is a static language used to define the structure and content of web pages.

JavaScript, on the other hand, is a programming language used for adding interactivity, dynamic behavior, and visual effects to web pages. It allows developers to create custom scripts that can manipulate the Document Object Model (DOM) of a web page, handle user events, and perform calculations and data manipulation.

JavaScript can also be used to create standalone web applications, mobile applications, and server-side applications, using frameworks such as Node.js.

For example, the following JavaScript code creates a button element and adds an event listener to it, so that when the button is clicked, an alert box appears with the message "Button clicked!":

```javascript
const button = document.createElement('button');
button.textContent = 'Click Me!';

button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  alert('Button clicked!');
});

document.body.appendChild(button);
```

In summary, HTML is a markup language used for defining the structure and content of web pages, while JavaScript is a programming language used for adding interactivity, dynamic behavior, and custom functionality to web pages and applications. HTML and JavaScript are often used together to create rich and engaging web experiences.

